    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Spanish moved Havana to its present harbour in 1519, and from 1561 the ships bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> were required to gather in its bay, which made it the main port of Spain’s American colonies.</li><li>Pirate attacks, beginning with the burning of 1555, led the Crown to fortify the city, and after the British siege of 1762 the Spanish made it the most heavily fortified city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> brought riches to the city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> stayed legal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> until 1886, and Havana became the capital of the independent Republic of Cuba in 1902 and the seat of the revolutionary government after 1959.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1763 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> gave Havana back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, less than a year after taking the city, and more than half of the British force that took it died, mostly of illness and in particular yellow fever.</p></aside>
    </section>

