    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1519</span><span class="rf-text">The town that became Havana is settled beside Puerto de Carenas, on the north coast</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1555</span><span class="rf-text">The French corsair Jacques de Sores takes Havana and burns much of it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1561</span><span class="rf-text">A royal decree requires all ships bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to assemble in Havana Bay</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1592</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> grants Havana the title of city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1762</span><span class="rf-text">A British fleet and army besiege and take Havana, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recovers in 1763</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1837</span><span class="rf-text">The first railway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> opens between Havana and Bejucal</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> relinquishes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> after the sinking of the USS Maine in Havana harbour</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1959</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution">Cuban Revolution</a> brings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a>’s government to power in Havana</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas.jpg" alt="Bartolomé de las Casas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fray_Bartolom%C3%A9_de_las_Casas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous / Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Havana lies on the northern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> along the Straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida-keys">Florida Keys</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>. Sebastián de Ocampo’s circumnavigation of the island in 1508 brought the first Spaniards to the place, and in 1510 the first Spanish colonists arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> and began the conquest of Cuba. In 1514 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</a> founded San Cristóbal de la Habana on the south coast, and an early map of 1514 places it at the mouth of the Mayabeque River. That settlement failed, mainly because the land around the river was low and swampy, and every Spanish attempt to found a town on that coast failed.</p><p>Between 1514 and 1519 the Spanish made settlements on the north coast. One of them, La Chorrera, stood beside the mouth of the Almendares River on higher and less marshy ground, and later became the neighbourhoods of Vedado and Miramar. The town that became Havana grew in 1519 next to what was then called Puerto de Carenas, ‘Careening Port’, and the quality of the harbour led to the change of site. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> wrote that ships had been careened there, which gave the port its name. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panfilo-de-narvaez">Pánfilo de Narváez</a> gave the town, the sixth founded in Cuba, the name San Cristóbal de la Habana. San Cristóbal is its patron saint, and Habana may come from Habaguanex, the chief of a local Taíno-speaking people about whom little else is known.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The harbour lay at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, with easy access to the Gulf Stream that navigators followed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and this led to its early growth as the principal port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>’s colonies in the New World. After the French attack of 1555 the Crown paid for fortresses to guard the main cities and to tighten its control of trade. A royal decree of 1561 then required every ship bound for Spain to assemble in Havana Bay. Ships arrived from May to August, waited for the best weather, and left together by September. Gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, emeralds, mahogany, leather, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, corn, manioc and cocoa passed through the port, and the gathered ships needed food, water and other goods from the city’s farms and workshops. In 1563 the captain general moved his residence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> to Havana.</p><p>On 20 December 1592 Philip II granted Havana the title of city, and the Crown later named it ‘Key to the New World and Rampart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>’. The city expanded greatly in the 17th century, building mostly in wood. By the middle of the 18th century it had more than seventy thousand inhabitants and was the third-largest city in the Americas, behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> and ahead of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. By 1740 it had Spain’s largest and most active shipyard and the only drydock in the New World, and El Arsenal built 109 ships, among them the Santísima <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, launched in 1769. The railway of 1837 carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> from the Güines valley to the harbour. Sugar and a flourishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> first brought riches to the city.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Havana&#8221; cites where it covers Ethnic breakdown, Poverty and slums:</p><ul><li>Dick Cluster, The History of Havana (Palgrave Macmillan, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus.jpg" alt="Christopher Columbus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_Man,_Said_to_be_Christopher_Columbus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sebastiano del Piombo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Havana was a town of the Spanish colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, and its city council was created in 1550 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of Cuba. The governor’s residence moved there in 1563, and the Captaincy General of Cuba, with its council, lasted from 1607 to 1898. In 1762 a British fleet and army took the city, which Sir George Keppel governed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763 returned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. Spain then rebuilt its defences. In 1796 the remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> were brought to Havana from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, and they stayed until 1898.</p><p>The war of Cuban independence from Spain had been under way for three years when the USS Maine sank in Havana harbour, and on 12 August 1898, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>, Spain relinquished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over Cuba. The island was then occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Cuba adopted a constitution in 1901, and in 1902 Havana became the capital of the new Republic of Cuba, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-estrada-palma">Tomás Estrada Palma</a> as its first president. In July 1940 it hosted the Havana Conference, at which the United States and 20 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> countries agreed to defend European colonies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> if their home countries fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>.</p><p>After the revolution of 1959 the municipal government was restructured with the rest of the state. The constitution of 1976 gave Havana the status of both a city and a province, and in 2010 the former province of La Habana was divided into Artemisa and Mayabeque.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> was virtually wiped out by Eurasian diseases in the earliest period of the Spanish conquest. Havana’s settlers were Spaniards, and Cuba received millions of immigrants during the Republic; it is estimated that one quarter of the Cuban population descends from Spanish immigrants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> and a flourishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> first brought riches to the city. In 1557 the city council banned freed black men from owning taverns or inns and from selling tobacco or wine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> stayed legal in Cuba until 1886. Chinese labourers, mostly Cantonese, were brought in the mid-19th century on work contracts of eight years, and many stayed. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> ended in 1865 former Confederates, among them the Confederate secretary of state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> P. Benjamin, came to Havana, and some former slaveholders went on running plantations there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2012 recorded 58.4% of the city’s people as white, 26.4% as mixed, 15.2% as black and 0.2% as Asian.</p><p>Roman Catholics form the largest religious group, and the cathedral, built from 1748 and completed in 1777, is dedicated to San Cristóbal. Before 1959 the Jewish community numbered more than 15,000, many of them refugees from Nazi persecution, and many left for Miami or Israel after 1959. The University of Havana was established in 1728, and the Gran Teatro opened in 1837. The city made rum, cigars and ships, and the walls built from 1674 to 1740 were knocked down in 1863 so that it could grow.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/luis-vicente-de-velasco"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luis-vicente-de-velasco.jpg" alt="Luis Vicente de Velasco" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/luis-vicente-de-velasco">Luis Vicente de Velasco</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luis_Vicente_de_Velasco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Nicolás de la Escalera, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first attack on Havana was by the French corsair Jacques de Sores in 1555, who took the city easily, plundered it and burned much of it, leaving without the great wealth he had hoped for. In 1649 an epidemic brought from Cartagena in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> affected a third of the population. A hurricane in 1730 destroyed the women’s hospital and the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> de Paula, which were rebuilt and enlarged in 1745.</p><p>The British siege began on 6 June 1762, when more than 50 ships and over 11,000 men landed east of the city. The British took the heights of La Punta and bombarded El Morro Castle and the city. El Morro fell on 30 July, after the death of its defender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-vicente-de-velasco">Luis Vicente de Velasco e Isla</a>, and the city surrendered on 13 August. The British lost 560 men to combat wounds, but more than half of their force ultimately died of illness, yellow fever in particular. In 1898 the sinking of the USS Maine in the harbour was the immediate cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>.</p><p>After 1959 all private property and industry were expropriated, and by 1966 to 1968 all privately owned businesses had been nationalised. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> imposed an embargo, and shortages hit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and Havana hard. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> collapsed in 1991 its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> to Cuba ended, and a severe downturn followed in the 1990s, known as the Special Period. Many buildings decayed, and partial or total collapses have injured and killed people. A tornado in January 2019 killed eight people and injured 195, and in 2022 at least 40 people died in an explosion at the Hotel Saratoga.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Old Havana was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1982. Its forts, among them El Morro, built between 1589 and 1630, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> de la Punta, the Real Fuerza and San Carlos de la Cabaña, which the Spanish began after 1763, record the years when the city guarded the route of the treasure fleet. The cathedral, built from 1748 to 1777, is the best example of Cuban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>. The Capitolio of 1929 and the Necrópolis Cristóbal Colón, built in 1876 and holding nearly a million tombs, belong to later periods, and Jean-Claude Nicolas Forestier, head of urban planning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, worked on the city’s master plan from 1925, though the Great Depression cut short many of his ideas.</p><p>The coat of arms shows the three castles of the city, the Fuerza, the Morro and the Punta, with a key for the gateway to the New World, the title the Crown gave it. Contemporary Havana is three cities in one: Old Havana, Vedado and the newer suburban districts. The city reached 1 million inhabitants in the 1943 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and its population was 2,142,939 in 2021 and 1,749,964 by the end of 2024. It is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the seat of its government, and it has 15 municipalities and 105 people’s councils.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/guantanamera">Guantanamera<span>c. 1929</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Havana was founded on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 1514 and settled at its present harbour in 1519. It became the assembly point of the Spanish treasure fleet, a fortress city taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1762, a port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and, from 1902, the capital of Cuba.</em></p>

